[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186054676,
      "id": "3123f8ed-a1ca-52b7-8427-2f3b60d9e68a",
      "title": "Identifying Points That Lie on a Line"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "342cb64e-4ad8-5b98-9e03-79c3f1b9dcfd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/e3cc5fe292934a512d789633855957f69f2a71e51a6fa1e97f88ce1ed348dbbf.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-3x+2$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(2,-4)$, $B(0,2)$, $C(4,-8)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(2,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x+2 \\\\ (-4) &= -3(2)+2 \\\\ -4 &= -4 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x+2 \\\\ (2) &= -3(0)+2 \\\\ 2 &= 2 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(4,-8)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x+2 \\\\ (-8) &= -3(4)+2 \\\\ -8 &= -10 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $B$ lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "593ac20f-daaf-5dac-b47f-1866e49b3710",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
