      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/05436b48c5ecaf8f48e648dc305be7cd69d0c4873eb3a7253c56c83ac1aa97bb.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x+4$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,6)$, $B(2,2)$, $C(-2,7)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+4 \\\\ (6) &= -2(-1)+4 \\\\ 6 &= 6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(2,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+4 \\\\ (2) &= -2(2)+4 \\\\ 2 &= 0 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-2,7)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+4 \\\\ (7) &= -2(-2)+4 \\\\ 7 &= 8 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $A$ lies on the line.",
