    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5970f561-8b98-5d08-b18d-0349229f45f0",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/ea4c9cb827ae179e000a99529bebda012de3e1fed9a3dd951e4b642efcedd505.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x+1$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,5)$, $B(-4,9)$, $C(-1,4)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+1 \\\\ (5) &= -2(-2)+1 \\\\ 5 &= 5 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-4,9)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+1 \\\\ (9) &= -2(-4)+1 \\\\ 9 &= 9 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-1,4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+1 \\\\ (4) &= -2(-1)+1 \\\\ 4 &= 3 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $B$ lie on the line.",
