      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/132e7e618e3ea25154f68803dfd7d82236d082ea3da6b97c1dfc48a47fc077a7.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x+6$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,4)$, $B(3,-9)$, $C(2,-3)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -5x+6 \\\\ (4) &= -5(0)+6 \\\\ 4 &= 6 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(3,-9)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -5x+6 \\\\ (-9) &= -5(3)+6 \\\\ -9 &= -9 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -5x+6 \\\\ (-3) &= -5(2)+6 \\\\ -3 &= -4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ lies on the line.",
