    "question": {
      ":question/id": "121185e3-f710-5533-a906-6c7eb1fcd9cb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/44aa338c30666661bf1c40360d5d6f9c44b9aa2ac5b604d135d9707abbe728b3.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x+5$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5)$, $B(2,3)$, $C(-1,7)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+5 \\\\ (5) &= -2(0)+5 \\\\ 5 &= 5 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(2,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+5 \\\\ (3) &= -2(2)+5 \\\\ 3 &= 1 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-1,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+5 \\\\ (7) &= -2(-1)+5 \\\\ 7 &= 7 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f2f11949-fbb8-5296-813f-484f08c1ce91",
