      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/dc66f3640cd05413703de606b558dbe60c4cbbf693009f897bbdf553ca12d1e5.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(2,-4)$, $B(-3,3)$, $C(0,0)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(2,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x \\\\ (-4) &= -2(2) \\\\ -4 &= -4 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-3,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x \\\\ (3) &= -2(-3) \\\\ 3 &= 6 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(0,0)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x \\\\ (0) &= -2(0) \\\\ 0 &= 0 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line.",
