    "question": {
      ":question/id": "34b42ebe-b4fd-54af-9eea-e1557a1908df",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/2c8602c20bc8fc2d1923f148f729fbace66b7a27af94404051a25774e31d1ff9.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x+2$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(3,-3)$, $B(1,-1)$, $C(-3,8)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(3,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+2 \\\\ (-3) &= -2(3)+2 \\\\ -3 &= -4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,-1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+2 \\\\ (-1) &= -2(1)+2 \\\\ -1 &= 0 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-3,8)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+2 \\\\ (8) &= -2(-3)+2 \\\\ 8 &= 8 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4e99599e-d5de-5148-96fc-b6c3a8b4c7ac",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
