    "question": {
      ":question/id": "93768d01-1920-59ce-bba9-3bd5260ca1ea",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/c487a007e0c9401ee7aeebaee0cc365708d3bd6e16a2d2feb780007374c9a245.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,0)$, $B(-1,5)$, $C(2,-9)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,0)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -5x \\\\ (0) &= -5(0) \\\\ 0 &= 0 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -5x \\\\ (5) &= -5(-1) \\\\ 5 &= 5 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-9)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -5x \\\\ (-9) &= -5(2) \\\\ -9 &= -10 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $B$ lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "11981895-bd94-57bf-a214-80f2005343e9",
