      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/b99180240d48e47d202c94b95e923126f4fcd730b47482c1601448f07b8b3a9c.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x+4$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,7)$, $B(-2,9)$, $C(-1,6)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,7)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+4 \\\\ (7) &= -2(-3)+4 \\\\ 7 &= 10 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-2,9)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+4 \\\\ (9) &= -2(-2)+4 \\\\ 9 &= 8 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-1,6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+4 \\\\ (6) &= -2(-1)+4 \\\\ 6 &= 6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "64d8c5d0-3a68-561a-bc95-9d87c1b00a42",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
