    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8499fce8-9eb8-5ae2-8844-f0859626e3b0",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/de4933cf0a0b8b08d1482bc64bb6d2681878de6cf57c686e223bba2abeaf0ed2.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(2,-3)$, $B(0,-3)$, $C(3,-6)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(2,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x \\\\ (-3) &= -2(2) \\\\ -3 &= -4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x \\\\ (-3) &= -2(0) \\\\ -3 &= 0 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(3,-6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x \\\\ (-6) &= -2(3) \\\\ -6 &= -6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "7a08a259-2726-57f2-a38c-f0c9f1ea25eb",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
