    "question": {
      ":question/id": "60a9c5ef-b6c2-5181-8910-c032f0fced2e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/5c84e35a377c5a6af119a5dfdacdf4a7fd3b58fb5d89c9f85825819984298d34.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-3x-1$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,2)$, $B(-4,8)$, $C(1,-4)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x-1 \\\\ (2) &= -3(-2)-1 \\\\ 2 &= 5 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-4,8)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x-1 \\\\ (8) &= -3(-4)-1 \\\\ 8 &= 11 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x-1 \\\\ (-4) &= -3(1)-1 \\\\ -4 &= -4 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line.",
