      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/09492ef4e20bb472a561e1024fe8d81c2ba98aabf16bbce463d5d2554e8ae4a0.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x+6$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(1,4)$, $B(3,-3)$, $C(0,9)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(1,4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+6 \\\\ (4) &= -2(1)+6 \\\\ 4 &= 4 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(3,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+6 \\\\ (-3) &= -2(3)+6 \\\\ -3 &= 0 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(0,9)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+6 \\\\ (9) &= -2(0)+6 \\\\ 9 &= 6 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $A$ lies on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "594eae1f-11c2-5cea-a329-51b5500c2a01",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
