      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/5f559f5afe7e15e3812b07dbca99dd909d134df347f4740499e172587b9c824f.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x-3$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(1,-8)$, $B(3,-9)$, $C(-2,3)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(1,-8)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-3 \\\\ (-8) &= -2(1)-3 \\\\ -8 &= -5 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(3,-9)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-3 \\\\ (-9) &= -2(3)-3 \\\\ -9 &= -9 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-2,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-3 \\\\ (3) &= -2(-2)-3 \\\\ 3 &= 1 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ lies on the line.",
